.TH smi-project-cleanup "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Project Command"
.SH NAME
smi-project-cleanup \- clean up and rename a new project made from a template
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-project-cleanup
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsmi-project-cleanup\fR command is run in the root folder of a new project, normally one made by
\fBsmi-project-prepare\fR(1). It takes no arguments. The project says what to do in the file
\fB.smi-project-cleanup\fR of that folder, a shell file that the command includes.
.PP
The command runs two stages, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
\fBcleanup\fR: the function \fBdoCleanup\fR of the file, removing what the new project does not need.
.IP 2. 4
\fBrename\fR: the function \fBdoRename\fR of the file, renaming the template names to the new project.
.IP 3. 4
\fBpost\fR: the function \fBdoPost\fR of the file, what comes after both, such as removing the
\fB.smi-project-cleanup\fR files themselves.
.PP
A stage whose function the file does not define is skipped, so a project implements only what it needs.
The command stops on the first failing command. Without a \fB.smi-project-cleanup\fR file there is nothing
to clean up: the command says so and exits with status 0.
.SH "THE .smi-project-cleanup FILE"
.PP
\fBdoCleanup\fR and \fBdoRename\fR call one small function per step through \fBprojectStep\fR, which
prints the stage and the step name before it runs it, so the output lists every step done. The helper
functions of the library \fBproject.sh\fR are available:
.TP
.B projectName
The name of the new project: the name of the current folder.
.TP
.BI projectStep " FUNCTION " [ ARGUMENT ...]
Prints the stage and the step name, and runs it.
.TP
.BI projectRemove " PATH" ...
Removes files and folders recursively, a missing one is no error.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveExcept " KEEP PATH" ...
Removes every \fIPATH\fR whose file name is not \fIKEEP\fR.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveEmptyFolders " FOLDER" ...
Removes each \fIFOLDER\fR that is empty, in the order given, so a folder emptied by the one before it
goes too.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveEmptyFile " FILE"
Removes \fIFILE\fR when it is empty.
.TP
.BI projectRename " FROM TO"
Renames \fIFROM\fR to \fITO\fR when \fIFROM\fR exists.
.TP
.BI projectReplaceInFile " FILE FROM TO"
Replaces every \fIFROM\fR (a sed regular expression, without |) with \fITO\fR in \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
.BI projectReplaceWordInFile " FILE FROM TO"
Replaces \fIFROM\fR, taken literally, with \fITO\fR in \fIFILE\fR only where it stands as a whole word:
neither the character before nor the one after is a letter, digit, _ or \-. So app is renamed in
app.jar or /app/, but not in my\-app or app\-api, and running it again changes nothing.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveXmlElement " FILE TAG " [ TEXT ]
Removes each element <\fITAG\fR>...</\fITAG\fR> of \fIFILE\fR that starts on a line of its own, or
with \fITEXT\fR only those that contain \fITEXT\fR, such as one plugin of a pom by its artifactId.
.TP
.BI projectWriteFile " FILE CONTENT"
Writes \fICONTENT\fR into \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
.BI projectDeleteLinesInFile " FILE PATTERN" ...
Deletes the lines of \fIFILE\fR that match a \fIPATTERN\fR (a sed regular expression, without |).
.TP
.BI projectEditFile " FILE COMMAND " [ ARGUMENT ...]
Runs \fICOMMAND\fR, such as sed or awk, with \fIFILE\fR as standard input and writes its output back
into \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveMavenModulesExcept " DIRECTORY KEEP" ...
Removes every module of \fIDIRECTORY\fR/pom.xml that is not a \fIKEEP\fR: its <module> line and its folder.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveMavenModulesWithoutFolder " DIRECTORY"
Removes the <module> lines of \fIDIRECTORY\fR/pom.xml whose module folder does not exist.
.TP
.BI projectRenameMavenArtifact " FROM TO POM" ...
Renames the artifactId and the name \fIFROM\fR to \fITO\fR in every \fIPOM\fR: the module itself and the
dependencies on it, so the new project installs its own artifacts beside the template ones in the local
Maven repository instead of overwriting them.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveParagraphsInFile " FILE FIRST_LINE" ...
Removes the paragraphs of \fIFILE\fR, the lines up to and with the next empty line, whose first line is
exactly \fIFIRST_LINE\fR, such as a .gitignore section by its comment line.
.TP
.BI projectRemoveGitignoreEntriesWithoutFolder " " [ FILE ]
Removes the entries of \fIFILE\fR, default .gitignore, that are paths into a folder that does not exist,
such as removed/build/ when removed is gone. Patterns without a folder, such as target/ or **/*.log, stay.
A section, the lines between empty lines, whose entries are all removed goes with its comment lines.
.TP
.B projectRemoveGitSubmodulesWithoutFolder
Removes the entries of .gitmodules whose path does not exist, and .gitmodules when it is left empty.
.PP
Every helper edits nothing when its file is missing, so the steps can be run again.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A .smi-project-cleanup that removes the folder attic and renames the folder example-app to the project
name:
.PP
.nf
removeAttic() {
    projectRemove attic
}

renameApp() {
    projectRename example-app "$(projectName)"
}

doCleanup() {
    projectStep removeAttic
}

doRename() {
    projectStep renameApp
}
.fi
.PP
Run in the project root folder:
.PP
.B cd myproject && smi-project-cleanup
.PP
It prints:
.PP
.nf
cleanup: removeAttic
rename: renameApp
.fi
